101
00:08:05.394 --> 00:08:06.269
Part 8.

102
00:08:06.549 --> 00:08:07.658
Common mistakes.

103
00:08:08.908 --> 00:08:13.985
Searching only the user's own cell, which misses places 10 m away across the boundary.

104
00:08:14.265 --> 00:08:17.124
Choosing the precision without considering the radius.

105
00:08:17.404 --> 00:08:22.289
The cell must be at least as large as the search radius, or 9 cells won't cover it.

106
00:08:22.569 --> 00:08:24.447
Comparing distances in degrees.

107
00:08:24.727 --> 00:08:28.631
One degree of longitude shrinks with latitude, so use haversine.

108
00:08:28.911 --> 00:08:30.511
Hot cells in dense cities.

109
00:08:30.791 --> 00:08:34.929
Split adaptively (quadtree) or use precision that varies with density.

110
00:08:36.279 --> 00:08:37.175
Part 9.

111
00:08:37.455 --> 00:08:38.799
Follow-up questions.

112
00:08:40.049 --> 00:08:40.924
Question.

113
00:08:41.204 --> 00:08:44.425
Geohash versus quadtree versus S2 or H3?

114
00:08:44.705 --> 00:08:46.071
Thirty-second answer:

115
00:08:46.351 --> 00:08:54.777
Geohash is a string prefix that works with any key-value store or B-tree, but its cells are rectangles that are distorted near the poles.

116
00:08:55.057 --> 00:09:00.199
A quadtree adapts to density (it splits busy areas) but is an in-memory structure.

117
00:09:00.479 --> 00:09:09.332
S2 (Google) and H3 (Uber) use spherical or hexagonal cells with uniform neighbours, which is better for ride-hailing and analytics.

118
00:09:10.682 --> 00:09:11.557
Question.

119
00:09:11.837 --> 00:09:14.482
